#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# bin_lists

#% text
# `bin_lists(xs, w)` gives the lists of the real numbers of `xs` in the bins `[k w, (k + 1) w)` from the lowest to the highest occupied; `bin_lists(xs, [a, b, dx])` uses the bins `[a, a + dx)`, ... that lie within `[a, b]` (`[a, b]` alone: steps of 1); `bin_lists(xs, [[e1, e2, ...]])` the bins `[e1, e2)`, `[e2, e3)`, ....

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Elements that have no real numerical value are left out.

#% item
# The bins are those of `bin_counts`. The specification `[a, b, dx]` (dx > 0) means the bins `[a + k dx, a + (k + 1) dx)` that lie within `[a, b]`: there are floor((b - a)/dx) of them, so a step that does not divide `b - a` leaves the part of `[a, b]` above the last edge out, a step larger than `b - a` gives no bins, and `b` itself is outside the last bin. For machine numbers a quotient within a relative 10^-12 of an integer counts as that integer, and the last edge is then `b`. `bin_lists`, `histogram` and `histogram_list` read the specification the same way.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], 2)
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [3, 2, 2], [4, 5], [6]]

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [0, 10, 1])
#% output
#= [[], [1, 1], [2, 2], [3], [4], [5], [6], [], [], []]

#% cell
bin_lists([1, 3, 2, 1, 4, 5, 6, 2], [[-inf, 2, 5, 7, inf]])
#% output
#= [[1, 1], [3, 2, 4, 2], [5, 6], []]

